1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,359
Look at this code for a moment. Those
lines appear only after the morning
booking wave — never during deploy. The
Redis rate-cache pool was empty until the
first requests forced connections open.

2
00:00:11,479 --> 00:00:24,399
Overnight shutdowns were worse: Redis
logged abrupt disconnects because nobody
closed the client. The team does not need
another caching library. They need
lifecycle hooks: warm before traffic,
close on stop.

3
00:00:24,519 --> 00:00:28,599
Spring's bean lifecycle is a sequence, not
a single new.

4
00:00:28,719 --> 00:00:41,159
Rough order for a managed singleton:
instantiate (constructor), populate
properties / inject collaborators, aware
interfaces (BeanNameAware,
BeanFactoryAware, ApplicationContextAware,
…),

5
00:00:41,279 --> 00:00:57,519
BeanPostProcessor.postProcessBeforeInitial
ization, init callbacks (@PostConstruct,
InitializingBean.afterPropertiesSet,
custom init-method), BeanPostProcessor.pos
tProcessAfterInitialization (AOP proxies
often wrap here),

6
00:00:57,639 --> 00:01:05,000
bean in service, then on context close
@PreDestroy / DisposableBean.destroy /
custom destroy-method.

7
00:01:05,119 --> 00:01:18,519
You do not memorize every extension point
on day one. You learn where your pool open
and close belong — after injection, before
traffic; on shutdown, before the JVM
exits. Look at this java for a moment.

8
00:01:18,639 --> 00:01:28,439
Runtime after the fix. During context
refresh, Spring creates RateCachePool via
the constructor — only the URI string is
set; factory and template are still null.

9
00:01:28,559 --> 00:01:36,439
Dependency injection for this bean is
already done (the @Value resolved through
Environment). Aware callbacks run if
implemented.

10
00:01:36,559 --> 00:01:45,839
Then init: afterPropertiesSet builds the
Lettuce factory, starts it, builds the
template, and pings to force a real
connection before the web tier accepts
traffic.

11
00:01:45,959 --> 00:01:57,719
In a typical Boot app, context refresh
completes before Tomcat starts serving —
so the morning wave hits a warm pool
instead of reconnect warnings. Controllers
call get; the template is ready.

12
00:01:57,839 --> 00:02:08,719
On SIGTERM, Boot closes the context in
reverse dependency order where possible,
calls destroy on disposables, and Redis
sees an orderly shutdown instead of TCP
resets.

13
00:02:08,839 --> 00:02:19,319
Swap @PostConstruct for afterPropertiesSet
if you prefer annotations; the timing
relative to injection is what matters —
init runs after dependencies are set.

14
00:02:19,439 --> 00:02:29,559
Prefer one init style per bean to avoid
double-open surprises when both
@PostConstruct and afterPropertiesSet do
real work. Failure mode symptoms from the
hotel rate desk.

15
00:02:29,679 --> 00:02:42,079
Init that opens Redis against a down host:
BeanCreationException during refresh,
process fails to start — better than
serving 500s with an empty pool, unless
you deliberately lazy-init and degrade.

16
00:02:42,199 --> 00:02:50,159
Init that assumes another bean is already
warmed without depends-on or constructor
injection: intermittent NPEs when creation
order shifts.

17
00:02:50,280 --> 00:02:58,840
Destroy skipped because the bean was never
registered as disposable (raw new
RateCachePool outside the container) —
Redis still sees hard disconnects.

18
00:02:58,960 --> 00:03:05,080
Calling get from a constructor or from a
BeanPostProcessor that runs before init:
template still null.

19
00:03:05,200 --> 00:03:15,080
Prototype-scoped beans get init per
instance but destroy only if you asked the
container to manage the instance —
forgotten destroy on prototypes is a
classic leak.

20
00:03:15,200 --> 00:03:27,680
Another tell: @PreDestroy methods that
never run because the JVM was killed with
kill -9, or because a non-daemon thread
kept the process alive past an incomplete
context close. Trade-offs.

21
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:41,840
Interface callbacks (InitializingBean /
DisposableBean) couple your class to
Spring APIs; @PostConstruct / @PreDestroy
need a common annotation processor but
keep the class freer of Spring types;

22
00:03:41,960 --> 00:03:47,879
custom init/destroy method names in
definitions suit third-party classes you
cannot annotate.

23
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:58,400
Lifecycle hooks are the right place for
scarce resources — pools, file watchers,
subscription threads. They are the wrong
place for request-scoped business logic.

24
00:03:58,520 --> 00:04:06,520
Lazy-init delays the cost until first use;
for Redis rate cache that recreates the
morning reconnect storm unless something
else warms it.

25
00:04:06,640 --> 00:04:16,520
Misconception unique to lifecycle:
"@PostConstruct runs before dependency
injection finishes." It does not.
Injection completes first; then init
callbacks.

26
00:04:16,639 --> 00:04:27,599
If your init method sees null
collaborators, the bug is elsewhere —
wrong injection, wrong bean, field access
before the container finished, or you
constructed the object yourself outside
Spring.

27
00:04:27,719 --> 00:04:43,359
Another misconception: "Constructor body
is the init hook." Constructors run during
instantiate, before collaborators are
injectable via setters or some field
paths; constructor injection is safe for
final deps,

28
00:04:43,479 --> 00:04:51,959
but opening external resources in a
constructor makes testing and proxy
subclassing harder — prefer init callbacks
for side-effecting startup.

29
00:04:52,079 --> 00:05:04,599
Pools warm cleanly now. The bank batch
team watching from across the hall still
configures the same lifecycle idea in
2012-era XML, and they want a migration
path toward Java config without a big-bang
rewrite.

30
00:05:04,719 --> 00:05:09,799
How you express definitions — not only
when they init — becomes the next fight.
